      "title": "Main ___ hoon = I am ___",
      "body": "In Urdu the verb goes LAST. English says \"I am happy\"; Urdu says \"I happy am\" — Main khush hoon. Put any describing word in the gap. Add bohat before it for \"very\", and bhi after main for \"too\".",
      "examples": [
        "s-b1-1",
        "s-b1-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Aap ___ hain = You are ___",
      "body": "Main goes with hoon; aap goes with hain. To ask a yes/no question, just put Kya at the start. To say \"not\", put nahi right before the verb: Main theek nahi hoon.",
      "examples": [
        "s-b2-1",
        "s-b2-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Yeh mera ___ hai = This is my ___",
      "body": "Hai means \"is\" and goes last. \"My\" changes to match the thing: mera for masculine words (ghar, bhai, naam), meri for feminine words (behen). \"Your\" is aap ka: aap ka naam = your name. And kya in the middle of a sentence means \"what\": Aap ka naam kya hai? = your name what is?",
      "examples": [
        "s-b3-1",
        "s-b3-2"
